<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latgalians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Latgallians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Latgalians were a Baltic tribe of eastern Latvia, settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> and in the uplands to the east by the late first millennium. Their lands included the principalities of Jersika, Koknese and Talava, and some had become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians under Rus' influence. Conquered by the German crusaders in the 13th century, they merged with neighbouring Baltic peoples into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latgalians.jpg" alt="Latgalians" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Tribes_c_1200.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Latgalians were an Eastern Baltic tribe whose origin is little known. In the 5th and 6th centuries they lived in the eastern part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a>, west of the Aiviekste River, and later in nearly all of that region. Written sources mention them from the 11th century onwards. They likely spoke a variant of the Latvian language, which probably became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of present-day Latvia during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a> because of their alliance with the crusaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Eastern Orthodoxy began to spread in Latgalian lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> in the 11th century, and in the 12th century Latgalian lands and their rulers paid tribute to the dukes of Polotsk. In the first two decades of the 13th century the Western Latgalians allied with German, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a>, crusaders. Their lands, the Eldership of Tālava and the principalities of Jersika and Koknese, were incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states. During the Livonian crusade Latgalian elders switched from Eastern Orthodoxy to Roman Catholicism and became vassals of the Livonian Order.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> left many regions of Semigallia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> depopulated, and part of the Latgalians migrated there during and after the war. Their characteristic material culture disappeared by the 16th century as they lost cultural differences with other inhabitants and evolved into present-day ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>About 80 flat cemeteries of Western Latgalian origin have been excavated, with more than two thousand burials. The Ludza Odukalns Cemetery (1890–1891) held 339 Late Iron Age burials, and the Aglonas Kristapiņi Cemetery, in use from the late 8th century to the 12th, 315. In the east, flat and barrow cemeteries both date from the 6th and 7th centuries, and in the 9th and 10th centuries a transition from flat graves to barrows began.</p><p>Dwellings included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a>, settlements and lake fortresses. The best researched hill fort is Jersika, occupied from the 10th to the 14th centuries, which forms a complex with Dignāja Hillfort across the river; Dignāja had been inhabited since the 6th century. The Āraiši lake fortress of the 9th century was excavated and reconstructed in the 1960s and 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latgalians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under masters Wenno and Volquin they subdued the Livs, <b>Latgalians</b> and Estonians, often quarrelling with the bishops and with Denmark over the spoils.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a></li><li><blockquote>Jersika was a hill fort on the Daugava in eastern Latvia and the centre of a <b>Latgalian</b> principality linked to the Principality of Polotsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-jersika">Principality of Jersika</a></li><li><blockquote>Koknese, called Kukenois by the Germans, was a fortified centre on the Daugava in what is now Latvia, inhabited by <b>Latgalians</b> and Selonians.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-koknese">Principality of Koknese</a></li><li><blockquote>It bordered the Livonians and <b>Latgalians</b> to the south, with whom it traded and fought, and appears in detail in the Livonian Chronicle of Henry from the early 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a></li><li><blockquote>The Selonians settled in what is now south-eastern Latvia and north-eastern Lithuania during the first millennium, between the Semigallians and the <b>Latgalians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/selonians">Selonians</a></li><li><blockquote>Tālava was a <b>Latgalian</b> territory in what is now north-eastern Latvia, with its main stronghold at Beverīna.</blockquote><a href="/state/talava">Tālava</a></li></ul></section>
